  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\n- Cool and dry again tomorrow\n\nIn spite of the persistence of anomalous ridging aloft,\ntemperatures are a bit more seasonable this afternoon behind this\nmorning's weak cold front, albeit technically still a few degrees\nabove average overall. We've also seen a modest increase in cloud\ncover across primarily the northern lower peninsula thanks to a \nplume of mid-level moisture, but this has largely remained to our\nnorth, and is not producing any precipitation aside from a few \npockets of virga. Overnight, surface winds will slowly veer to the\neast, which should maintain weak cold air advection and lead to a\nchilly morning in the mid 30s to low 40s, in spite of some lingering\ncloud cover. Surface winds will continue to slowly veer to the \nsoutheast tomorrow, but cooler low level air will remain in place \nfor one more day of seasonable, fall-like temperatures. \nPrecipitation is also not expected tomorrow as we await another \nweak cold front late Wednesday and Thursday. \n\n- Warmer mid-week, with shower chances Thursday\n\nBetween Wednesday and Thursday, a synoptic scale ridge aloft will\nremain firmly in place across the eastern U.S., and a weakening \nshortwave and associated surface low will move around its \nperiphery across southern Canada. This will bring the return of\nsouth to southwesterly winds and modest warm air advection\nWednesday, followed by a diminishing cold front Wednesday night\nand Thursday. The former is expected to drive another warming \ntrend and bring temperatures back to comfortably average values \nWednesday afternoon (low 70s). Meanwhile, confidence is increasing\nthat the weakening front will bring a round of light showers to\nthe area Thursday, although precipitation amounts should be quite\nmodest (10-30% chance of more than 1/4 inch). There remain some \nhints of very weak instability as well, so we can't rule out a \nweak rumble of thunder or two, either. Similarly warm temperatures\ncan also be expected Thursday, although perhaps a few degrees\ncooler thanks to the increasing cloud cover. \n\n- Rain chances steadily increase this weekend onward\n\nConfidence in the day-to-day details decreases considerably Friday\nonwards, thanks to the gradual development of a more unsettled \npattern and increasingly divergent ensemble members. The \naforementioned weak cold front is expected to sink south of the \narea sometime Thursday, but anomalous ridging aloft will be \ndifficult to dislodge over the weekend, and this front will \nlikewise not be followed by a significant surge of cooler air.\nHowever, shortwaves will continue to impinge on the western\nperiphery of the ridge over the weekend, and shower chances are\nexpected to increase in kind. While this gradual pattern\nshift does favor an overall increase in precipitation chances over\nthe weekend, confidence in the timing details remains is very \nlow, and ensemble probabilities peak at only 30-40% on Sunday.\nThis does leave open a low-end potential (20-30%) for rain on\nHalloween (Saturday), although currently rain remains slightly \nfavored on the following day.\n\nEarly next weekend onward, there continues to be in increasing \nsignal for a deeper wave and associated storm system, although \ncluster analysis reveals drastic differences in the timing and \nstrength of such a system at this time range (8+ days out). \nWhile we may not be able to nail down the daily specifics just \nyet, confidence is relatively high that our current pattern will \nbreak down sometime next week, bringing the return of more active \nweather and increasing rain chances.",
